# Sandbox design presentation

**October 27 freshness notice:** the user selected VM per lab, selective JiuwenBox reuse and an OpenJiuwen compatibility/contribution target. [[Designs/Sandbox Lab Design]] contains checkpoint 2. This deck remains the October 26 presentation; its placement and reuse explanation needs checkpoint 3 reconciliation before presenting it as the selected architecture.

[Open the live editable deck](page://page_7a233186a3548191869c85106fc552b4). Six main slides and two hidden backups, approximately six minutes. Timing is an estimate, not an aloud rehearsal.

The presentation uses the value-first structure reviewed in final Paper2Agent rev8 and Nuwa rev3: value, mechanism, worked example, AI4R fit, and a bounded next step. It borrows their compact diagrams, editable shapes, controlled indigo/teal palette and supporting notes without transferring their evidence to this design.

| Slide | What the audience learns |
|---|---|
| 1 Sandbox labs | Persistent goals, revisable strategy and fixed checked attempts preserve useful work |
| 2 Workload example | A late counter-semantics discovery changes the next experiment |
| 3 Lab architecture | Human authority, protected binding, bounded launch, independent checks and durable state have explicit owners |
| 4 Revision behavior | Unstarted, running, completed and uncertain work have different handling |
| 5 AI4R fit | Our frozen-plan baseline and upstream goal/replay source are distinct; integration work remains |
| 6 First delivery | One real adaptation, recovery, and matched planning-policy comparison can test the value |
| 7 Hidden backup | Permissions, stale inputs, crash, output rejection, updates, storage and stop behavior |
| 8 Hidden backup | Evidence scope and unresolved implementation decisions |

## Completeness and claim review

The talk is ready as a **design proposal**. It does not claim that adaptive M2 execution, cross-batch reuse, concurrent graph mutation, day-long autonomy or scientific improvement already exists. The example is explicitly illustrative. The source evidence and limitations are on the relevant slides and in speaker notes. The factory creates candidates, independent qualification owns eligibility, and central adoption stays separate. Existing qualified review profiles retain their own payload boundaries.

The main implementation gap is accepted-result transfer between batches: M1 proves same-run frozen-plan identity. M2 must define artifact identity, original acceptance, scope, dependency validity and authorized reads before enabling reuse. The initial delivery revises at batch boundaries. Concurrent live patches remain conditional on need and require atomic revision checks and descendant invalidation. Those decisions are visible rather than implied to be solved.

Before benefit claims, agree the first investigation boundary, completion rubric and quality/cost tradeoff. Compare fixed, every-batch and selective planning under the same capabilities, model, permissions, task/discovery inputs, total budget and independent scoring. Include no-change tasks, failed prerequisites and interruption, then repeat on held-out cases. A single useful revision demonstrates feasibility only. Actual host limits, backup/restore and long-duration reliability need separate observed evidence.

All eight live slides were rendered and inspected individually. Review corrected an ambiguous architecture flow so binding precedes launch and checks follow execution, and fixed text/connector crowding. The three tables and diagram elements are native editable objects; the last two slides are hidden and all slides have speaker notes. Renders and layout snapshots are retained in `review/`. This review is not a PowerPoint application check or runtime test.

